WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.672
Error paths are a common telemetry leakage
point because exceptions accumulate

2
00:00:04.752 --> 00:00:07.513
context as they move through libraries and
services.

3
00:00:07.593 --> 00:00:11.212
An error object may expose SQL fragments,
filenames, internal hosts,

4
00:00:11.292 --> 00:00:15.035
URLs with query strings, upstream response
bodies, request fragments,

5
00:00:15.115 --> 00:00:16.219
or library metadata.

6
00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:20.532
Logging the error alongside a complete
Express request compounds that exposure.

7
00:00:20.612 --> 00:00:24.293
Inspect how each logger serializes errors;
passing an Error directly,

8
00:00:24.373 --> 00:00:28.667
placing it under a special property, or
spreading it may produce different fields.

9
00:00:28.747 --> 00:00:31.876
Separate the client's response from
internal diagnostics.

10
00:00:31.956 --> 00:00:36.251
A caller should receive a stable status
and message appropriate to its trust level,

11
00:00:36.331 --> 00:00:39.153
without stack traces or internal
dependency details.

12
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:42.607
Protected telemetry can retain more
information for operators,

13
00:00:42.687 --> 00:00:45.203
but it still follows the sensitive-data
policy.

14
00:00:45.283 --> 00:00:48.841
Build a deliberate error event with a
server-controlled event name,

15
00:00:48.921 --> 00:00:52.540
error category, request identifier,
affected resource identifier,

16
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:53.663
and safe error type.

17
00:00:53.743 --> 00:00:57.977
Include a stack trace for unexpected
programming defects when it materially aids

18
00:00:58.057 --> 00:01:01.553
diagnosis and when storage, access, and
retention are controlled.

19
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:04.824
Expected and unexpected failures need
different treatment.

20
00:01:04.904 --> 00:01:09.260
Routine validation rejection or
authorization denial generally does not require a

21
00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.732
full exception stack or an error-level
alert.

22
00:01:11.812 --> 00:01:15.248
Dependency timeout, unavailable service,
resource exhaustion,

23
00:01:15.328 --> 00:01:19.684
and programming defect should have
distinct categories because their response and

24
00:01:19.764 --> 00:01:20.991
alerting needs differ.

25
00:01:21.071 --> 00:01:25.365
Categorization reduces noise and makes
rate-based detection meaningful without

26
00:01:25.445 --> 00:01:26.672
exposing raw messages.

27
00:01:26.752 --> 00:01:31.292
The original exception can remain
available to tightly controlled diagnostic tooling

28
00:01:31.372 --> 00:01:32.722
only when policy permits.

29
00:01:32.802 --> 00:01:34.335
Review every error boundary,

30
00:01:34.415 --> 00:01:37.667
including hosted error reporters and trace
exception events.

31
00:01:37.747 --> 00:01:41.919
Scrubbing only application logs does not
protect telemetry copied by automatic

32
00:01:41.999 --> 00:01:42.980
instrumentation.

33
00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:46.987
Avoid recording full URLs when query
strings can contain sensitive values,

34
00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:51.423
and avoid upstream payloads unless a
specific diagnostic need and protection model

35
00:01:51.503 --> 00:01:52.239
justify them.

36
00:01:52.319 --> 00:01:56.000
Good error telemetry answers what failed,
where, for which operation,

37
00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.043
and under which correlation context;

38
00:01:58.123 --> 00:02:01.620
it does not reproduce the request merely
because failure occurred.
